The '''dragon egg''' is a gravity-affected [[block]] that can be obtained only once per [[world]] (or twice {{in|Bedrock}}<ref name=TwoEggs>{{bug|MCPE-51345}}</ref>) by defeating the [[ender dragon]]. It [[teleport]]s to avoid being {{Control|mined}}, but drops as an [[item]] if it falls onto a non-full block such as a [[torch]] or is pushed by a [[piston]].

== Obtaining ==
The dragon egg is available in the Creative inventory. It can be obtained with {{control|pick block}} or the {{cmd|give}} command, or placed in the world with commands such as {{cmd|setblock}} and {{cmd|fill}}.

=== Breaking ===
The dragon egg usually cannot be mined directly, as trying to do so causes it to [[Teleportation|teleport]] within a 31×15×31 volume centered on the egg, with locations toward the center more likely. If all air blocks in that area are filled so there is nowhere for the egg to teleport to, or if it fails to find an air block after 1,000 attempts at teleporting, it can be mined. 

The dragon egg is a gravity-affected block, and drops as an [[item (entity)|item]] when pushed by a [[piston]] or when it falls onto a block [[Solid block#Height|less than a full block tall]], such as a [[torch]] or a bottom [[slab]].

When destroyed by an [[explosion]], the block always drops as an item.

The dragon egg is not immune to destruction by the ender dragon, despite being produced by it.<ref name=":0">{{bug|MC-165218}}</ref>
{{breaking row|horizontal=1|Dragon Egg}}

=== Post-generation ===
{{IN|Java}}, a single dragon egg is generated at (X=0, Z=0) when the first [[ender dragon]] is defeated. The Y-coordinate is determined by the <code>MOTION_BLOCKING</code> heightmap, which locates the highest block at (X=0, Z=0) with a collision box:

* The dragon egg spawns directly on top of this block
* If a block with collision is placed at the maximum build height (Y=255), the dragon egg fails to generate, as the target position (Y=256) exceeds the world height limit
* Any non-motion-blocking block at that location (such as a button, torch, redstone wire, or air) is overwritten and replaced by the dragon egg

{{IN|Bedrock}}, two dragon eggs generate on top of the exit portal; one generates when the first ender dragon is defeated, while the other generates when the second ender dragon is defeated.<ref name="TwoEggs" />

== Usage ==
{{see also|Falling Block#Behavior}}
[[File:Dragon Egg Heatmap.png|right|thumb|250px|Heatmap of where a dragon egg lands after trying to mine it]]
[[File:Particles of a dragon egg.png|thumb|Particles of a dragon egg]]

The dragon egg, if there is no block below it, falls until it lands on the next available block. When it is being affected by gravity and falling, it exhibits a smooth falling animation.

It does not [[suffocate]] mobs or players when it falls and covers them, nor does it squash mobs or players like [[anvil]]s and [[stalactite]]s do.

The dragon egg can be placed on a non-solid block without falling.

Like other falling blocks, when the dragon egg is floating, it has black particles falling from it.

=== Light source ===
The dragon egg emits a [[light level]] of 1.

=== Teleporting ===
To cause the egg to teleport, press {{control|attack}} or {{control|use}} while in [[Survival]] or [[Adventure]] mode (or only {{control|use}} in [[Creative]]). It teleports to an air block nearby (up to seven blocks vertically and fifteen blocks horizontally), emitting portal particles, as [[Enderman|endermen]] and [[Nether Portal|nether portals]]. It may teleport into the air and subsequently fall to the ground since it forcefully obeys gravity. However, it always checks if there is ground beneath the teleport destination.

When the egg teleports, it creates a particle trail of particles from its old location to its new one.

If all available air blocks are filled, it is possible to break the block.

Dragon egg teleportation lacks teleportation sounds.<ref>{{bug|MC-166291|||Invalid}}</ref>

If egg teleportation finds an invalid location (such as below the world), it makes up to 999 additional attempts to find a valid location.

